1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,753
You'd never guess what made this sound.

2
00:00:01,773 --> 00:00:07,473
[NOAA recording]

3
00:00:07,858 --> 00:00:11,129
In 1997, it was heard across the Pacific, over

4
00:00:11,149 --> 00:00:14,032
five thousand kilometers away. What was it, actually?

5
00:00:14,052 --> 00:00:16,027
That's the first of six strange

6
00:00:16,047 --> 00:00:17,760
sounds recorded in the ocean.

7
00:00:17,780 --> 00:00:20,037
For each one, we'll hear the real recording,

8
00:00:20,057 --> 00:00:21,732
look at what scientists found, and give an

9
00:00:21,752 --> 00:00:25,287
honest verdict: solved, best guess, or still unknown.

10
00:00:25,307 --> 00:00:26,919
One quick note. Most of these sounds

11
00:00:26,939 --> 00:00:29,084
are too low for human ears. So

12
00:00:29,104 --> 00:00:30,709
the recordings you'll hear have been sped

13
00:00:30,729 --> 00:00:33,449
up, exactly as the scientists published them.

14
00:00:33,469 --> 00:00:35,251
First, how do you even hear the ocean?

15
00:00:35,271 --> 00:00:37,496
During the Cold War, the U.S. Navy built

16
00:00:37,516 --> 00:00:41,607
a network of underwater microphones, called hydrophones, to

17
00:00:41,627 --> 00:00:44,971
listen for Soviet submarines. It was called SOSUS.

18
00:00:44,991 --> 00:00:47,654
After the Cold War ended, the Navy partly opened

19
00:00:47,674 --> 00:00:51,106
that system to scientists. And NOAA, the U.S. ocean

20
00:00:51,126 --> 00:00:54,235
agency, added hydrophones of its own, to listen for

21
00:00:54,255 --> 00:00:57,546
undersea earthquakes, volcanoes, ice and whales.

22
00:00:57,566 --> 00:01:00,774
Sound travels incredibly well in water. So these

23
00:01:00,794 --> 00:01:03,956
microphones started picking up things nobody expected.

24
00:01:03,976 --> 00:01:05,283
Number one: the Bloop.

25
00:01:05,303 --> 00:01:08,428
In the summer of 1997, NOAA's hydrophones in the

26
00:01:08,448 --> 00:01:12,599
Pacific caught an extremely loud, very low sound. It

27
00:01:12,619 --> 00:01:14,281
rose in pitch over about a minute.

28
00:01:14,301 --> 00:01:15,781
It was loud enough to be picked up

29
00:01:15,801 --> 00:01:19,259
by sensors more than five thousand kilometers apart.

30
00:01:19,279 --> 00:01:21,184
The source was traced to a remote corner

31
00:01:21,204 --> 00:01:23,982
of the South Pacific, far from any coast.

32
00:01:24,002 --> 00:01:26,437
Here's what it sounds like, sped up sixteen times.

33
00:01:26,457 --> 00:01:37,057
[NOAA recording]

34
00:01:37,442 --> 00:01:39,529
Because it was so loud, people wondered if

35
00:01:39,549 --> 00:01:41,487
it came from an animal. Maybe a giant

36
00:01:41,507 --> 00:01:43,823
one that nobody had ever seen.

37
00:01:43,843 --> 00:01:45,567
For years, the Bloop was one

38
00:01:45,587 --> 00:01:48,162
of the ocean's most famous mysteries.

39
00:01:48,182 --> 00:01:49,857
Then the answer came from the ice.

40
00:01:49,877 --> 00:01:52,528
In 2008, NOAA scientists were tracking a

41
00:01:52,548 --> 00:01:56,356
huge iceberg, called A53a, as it broke

42
00:01:56,376 --> 00:01:58,239
apart near South Georgia Island.

43
00:01:58,259 --> 00:02:00,829
As the iceberg cracked and fractured, their

44
00:02:00,849 --> 00:02:04,274
hydrophones recorded icequakes. And the icequakes looked

45
00:02:04,294 --> 00:02:05,832
very similar to the Bloop.

46
00:02:05,852 --> 00:02:09,077
NOAA's conclusion: the Bloop was consistent with icequakes

47
00:02:09,097 --> 00:02:11,922
from large icebergs, most likely somewhere between the

48
00:02:11,942 --> 00:02:15,198
Bransfield Strait and the Ross Sea, off Antarctica.

49
00:02:15,218 --> 00:02:17,289
Not a monster. Just ice, cracking on

50
00:02:17,309 --> 00:02:19,046
a scale that's hard to imagine.

51
00:02:19,066 --> 00:02:21,418
Verdict: solved. It was breaking ice.

52
00:02:21,438 --> 00:02:23,720
Number two: Julia and the Slow Down.

53
00:02:23,740 --> 00:02:26,436
On the first of March, 1999, NOAA's

54
00:02:26,456 --> 00:02:28,849
hydrophones in the eastern Pacific recorded a

55
00:02:28,869 --> 00:02:32,383
long, moaning sound. Scientists named it Julia.

56
00:02:32,403 --> 00:02:34,349
Here it is, sped up sixteen times.

57
00:02:34,369 --> 00:02:44,969
[NOAA recording]

58
00:02:45,354 --> 00:02:48,761
Two years earlier, in May 1997, they had recorded

59
00:02:48,781 --> 00:02:51,751
another strange one. It slowly dropped in pitch over

60
00:02:51,771 --> 00:02:54,205
about seven minutes. They called it the Slow Down.

61
00:02:54,225 --> 00:03:02,425
[NOAA recording]

62
00:03:02,810 --> 00:03:03,940
Both sounds turned out to have

63
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:05,422
the same kind of source.

64
00:03:05,442 --> 00:03:08,743
A huge drifting iceberg runs into the seafloor.

65
00:03:08,763 --> 00:03:11,038
As it scrapes along the bottom and slowly

66
00:03:11,058 --> 00:03:13,216
grinds to a stop, it groans.

67
00:03:13,236 --> 00:03:15,686
For Julia, NOAA says the most likely source is

68
00:03:15,706 --> 00:03:18,819
a large iceberg that ran aground off Antarctica.

69
00:03:18,839 --> 00:03:21,013
And for the Slow Down, the falling pitch

70
00:03:21,033 --> 00:03:23,857
tells the story: the iceberg is slowing down

71
00:03:23,877 --> 00:03:25,840
as it drags across the seafloor.

72
00:03:25,860 --> 00:03:28,324
Verdict: solved. Icebergs running aground.

73
00:03:28,344 --> 00:03:30,002
Number three: the Bio-Duck.

74
00:03:30,022 --> 00:03:33,022
In the 1960s, submarine crews in the Southern Ocean

75
00:03:33,042 --> 00:03:36,662
started hearing a strange, repeating sound. It reminded them

76
00:03:36,682 --> 00:03:38,827
of a duck. So they called it the Bio-Duck.

77
00:03:38,847 --> 00:03:40,184
Here's a NOAA recording of it.

78
00:03:40,204 --> 00:03:41,281
This one is not sped up.

79
00:03:41,301 --> 00:03:51,101
[NOAA recording]

80
00:03:51,486 --> 00:03:53,910
For about fifty years, nobody could say what made

81
00:03:53,930 --> 00:03:56,356
it. It was so regular that it almost sounded

82
00:03:56,376 --> 00:04:00,196
mechanical. Some even suspected secret military devices.

83
00:04:00,216 --> 00:04:02,115
But there were clues. The sound came back

84
00:04:02,135 --> 00:04:05,176
every year, around the same months, near Antarctica.

85
00:04:05,196 --> 00:04:07,434
That looked a lot like a migrating animal.

86
00:04:07,454 --> 00:04:10,429
In February 2013, a research team took a

87
00:04:10,449 --> 00:04:13,537
small boat into Wilhelmina Bay, off the Antarctic

88
00:04:13,557 --> 00:04:16,642
Peninsula. Using a long pole, they stuck suction-cup

89
00:04:16,662 --> 00:04:19,537
recorders onto two Antarctic minke whales.

90
00:04:19,557 --> 00:04:21,444
They were the first acoustic tags

91
00:04:21,464 --> 00:04:23,547
ever attached to this species.

92
00:04:23,567 --> 00:04:25,343
And on one of the tags, just before the

93
00:04:25,363 --> 00:04:28,482
whale dived to feed, the Bio-Duck sound appeared.

94
00:04:28,502 --> 00:04:31,297
The study came out in 2014. The lead

95
00:04:31,317 --> 00:04:33,966
scientist, Denise Risch of NOAA, said her heart

96
00:04:33,986 --> 00:04:35,698
jumped a little when she found it.

97
00:04:35,718 --> 00:04:37,498
We still don't know exactly why the whales

98
00:04:37,518 --> 00:04:39,251
make it. But we know who does.

99
00:04:39,271 --> 00:04:42,099
Verdict: solved. It's Antarctic minke whales.

100
00:04:42,119 --> 00:04:43,552
Number four: the Whistle.

101
00:04:43,572 --> 00:04:46,505
On the seventh of July, 1997, a NOAA

102
00:04:46,525 --> 00:04:49,800
hydrophone in the eastern Pacific recorded a single,

103
00:04:49,820 --> 00:04:52,065
wavering tone. They named it the Whistle.

104
00:04:52,085 --> 00:04:54,031
Here it is, sped up sixteen times.

105
00:04:54,051 --> 00:05:06,251
[NOAA recording]

106
00:05:06,636 --> 00:05:08,829
NOAA says it's similar to sounds recorded

107
00:05:08,849 --> 00:05:11,674
from undersea volcanoes while they were erupting.

108
00:05:11,694 --> 00:05:14,969
The same array had picked up volcano sounds before,

109
00:05:14,989 --> 00:05:18,696
from the Mariana Islands, about fourteen thousand kilometers away.

110
00:05:18,716 --> 00:05:20,066
But there's a problem. To find where a

111
00:05:20,086 --> 00:05:22,124
sound came from, you need at least three

112
00:05:22,144 --> 00:05:25,002
hydrophones. The Whistle was heard by only one.

113
00:05:25,022 --> 00:05:26,172
So we know what it sounds like.

114
00:05:26,192 --> 00:05:27,855
We just can't point to the volcano.

115
00:05:27,875 --> 00:05:30,708
Verdict: best guess. Probably an undersea eruption,

116
00:05:30,728 --> 00:05:32,678
but the source was never located.

117
00:05:32,698 --> 00:05:34,544
Number five: the Upsweep.

118
00:05:34,564 --> 00:05:37,327
When NOAA started listening to the Navy's hydrophones

119
00:05:37,347 --> 00:05:40,429
in August 1991, this sound was already there.

120
00:05:40,449 --> 00:05:42,912
It's a long series of rising sweeps,

121
00:05:42,932 --> 00:05:45,457
each lasting several seconds. And it's loud

122
00:05:45,477 --> 00:05:47,527
enough to be heard across the Pacific.

123
00:05:47,547 --> 00:05:49,482
Here it is, sped up twenty times.

124
00:05:49,502 --> 00:06:01,702
[NOAA recording]

125
00:06:02,087 --> 00:06:04,374
At first, some thought it came from whales. But

126
00:06:04,394 --> 00:06:06,581
the sound was too regular for an animal.

127
00:06:06,601 --> 00:06:08,977
It also changes with the seasons. It usually

128
00:06:08,997 --> 00:06:11,605
peaks in spring and fall. But no one

129
00:06:11,625 --> 00:06:14,362
knows whether the source itself changes, or just

130
00:06:14,382 --> 00:06:16,569
the way sound travels through the ocean.

131
00:06:16,589 --> 00:06:18,706
Scientists traced it roughly to a spot

132
00:06:18,726 --> 00:06:20,576
in the far South Pacific, near an

133
00:06:20,596 --> 00:06:23,209
area of suspected volcanic activity.

134
00:06:23,229 --> 00:06:25,887
And here's the strange part. Since 1991, it

135
00:06:25,907 --> 00:06:28,564
has been getting quieter. But it hasn't stopped.

136
00:06:28,584 --> 00:06:30,952
NOAA's own description ends with this: the

137
00:06:30,972 --> 00:06:33,104
origin of the sound is unresolved.

138
00:06:33,124 --> 00:06:36,651
Verdict: still unknown. Volcanic activity is the leading

139
00:06:36,671 --> 00:06:39,229
idea, but nobody has found the source.

140
00:06:39,249 --> 00:06:41,507
Number six: the loneliest whale.

141
00:06:41,527 --> 00:06:44,923
In 1989, a team from the Woods Hole Oceanographic

142
00:06:44,943 --> 00:06:47,996
Institution heard a whale call in the North Pacific.

143
00:06:48,016 --> 00:06:49,803
It had all the patterns of a whale.

144
00:06:49,823 --> 00:06:51,759
But it was at fifty-two hertz. Blue

145
00:06:51,779 --> 00:06:53,817
and fin whales call much lower, at

146
00:06:53,837 --> 00:06:56,049
around fifteen to twenty-five hertz.

147
00:06:56,069 --> 00:06:58,066
Here's the call, sped up ten times.

148
00:06:58,086 --> 00:07:10,286
[NOAA recording]

149
00:07:10,671 --> 00:07:12,959
After the Navy opened its hydrophones, the

150
00:07:12,979 --> 00:07:15,874
team tracked this one voice every year.

151
00:07:15,894 --> 00:07:18,670
It showed up between August and December, roamed from

152
00:07:18,690 --> 00:07:21,965
California to the Aleutian Islands of Alaska, and traveled

153
00:07:21,985 --> 00:07:25,023
up to eleven thousand kilometers in a single season.

154
00:07:25,043 --> 00:07:27,642
And in twelve years of careful listening, they found

155
00:07:27,662 --> 00:07:30,558
only one source with that call each season.

156
00:07:30,578 --> 00:07:32,604
In their words: it is perhaps difficult to

157
00:07:32,624 --> 00:07:34,624
accept that if this was a whale, there

158
00:07:34,644 --> 00:07:36,474
could have been only one of this kind

159
00:07:36,494 --> 00:07:38,994
in this large oceanic expanse.

160
00:07:39,014 --> 00:07:41,089
The press called it the loneliest whale

161
00:07:41,109 --> 00:07:43,504
in the world. It even inspired songs,

162
00:07:43,524 --> 00:07:47,120
including one by BTS, called Whalien 52.

163
00:07:47,140 --> 00:07:49,972
But is it really alone? In 2010, sensors off

164
00:07:49,992 --> 00:07:52,872
California picked up calls suggesting there might be more

165
00:07:52,892 --> 00:07:55,837
than one whale calling at fifty-two hertz.

166
00:07:55,857 --> 00:07:57,769
So what is it? The scientists suggested it

167
00:07:57,789 --> 00:08:00,253
might have a malformation, or be a hybrid

168
00:08:00,273 --> 00:08:02,704
of two species, like a blue whale and

169
00:08:02,724 --> 00:08:05,793
a fin whale. A 2021 documentary filmed a

170
00:08:05,813 --> 00:08:08,584
possible hybrid. But no one has ever confirmed

171
00:08:08,604 --> 00:08:10,230
which animal is making the call.

172
00:08:10,250 --> 00:08:12,252
Verdict: still unknown. We know it's a

173
00:08:12,272 --> 00:08:13,829
whale. We just don't know what kind.

174
00:08:13,849 --> 00:08:15,407
So, let's check the scoreboard.

175
00:08:15,427 --> 00:08:17,517
The Bloop: solved. Breaking ice.

176
00:08:17,537 --> 00:08:21,128
Julia and the Slow Down: solved. Icebergs running aground.

177
00:08:21,148 --> 00:08:24,069
The Bio-Duck: solved. Antarctic minke whales.

178
00:08:24,089 --> 00:08:26,892
The Whistle: best guess. An undersea volcano.

179
00:08:26,912 --> 00:08:28,720
The Upsweep: still unknown.

180
00:08:28,740 --> 00:08:31,379
The 52-hertz whale: still unknown.

181
00:08:31,399 --> 00:08:33,993
Three solved, one best guess, and two we still

182
00:08:34,013 --> 00:08:36,958
can't explain. The ocean covers most of our planet,

183
00:08:36,978 --> 00:08:38,390
and we're still learning to hear it.

184
00:08:38,410 --> 00:08:40,209
All recordings are from NOAA. The 3D

185
00:08:40,229 --> 00:08:43,041
scenes are illustrations based on the sources.

186
00:08:43,061 --> 00:08:44,599
Which sound surprised you the most?

187
00:08:44,619 --> 00:08:45,794
Tell me in the comments.
